# Keywords

Print vocabulary is the whole of [`docs/KEYWORDS.md`](docs/KEYWORDS.md). The **Rules** tab shows its player sections (HTML comments are agent-only). The game that print describes is [`docs/GAME_STATE.md`](docs/GAME_STATE.md). How it plays is [`docs/RULEBOOK.md`](docs/RULEBOOK.md).

When authoring cards, new mechanics, hooks, tokens, keywords, or other rules print:

1. Read `docs/KEYWORDS.md` before writing print or effect data.
2. Use an operator that file already names. Prefer `[Mark N X]`, `[Strip N X]`, and `[Modify]` for a new token or a change to a roll, a die, a moveset, a target, or a Tag. Do not mint a verb. Block, Counter, Parry, and Tag already have print in the glossary. Dodge is not current print.
3. A new token joins **X** on `[Mark]` / `[Strip]`. It does not get its own keyword. Shield is not a resource.
4. Do not print `[Forge]`, `[Requires]`, `[Spend]`, `[Generate]`, `[Empower]`, `[Pierce]`, `[Convert]`, `[Desynthesize]`, or an attribute-only verb. `[Block N]` is the print for lowering damage.
5. Timing stays a prefix, as the glossary writes it (`On roll:`, `On attack:`). Never “Whenever…”.
6. The change is not done when print and the glossary disagree. A keyword you introduce is missing until it is in `docs/KEYWORDS.md`. A keyword in that file that no current Tag Skirmish card uses is extra until you remove it, unless the user explicitly kept it. `[Mark]`, `[Strip]`, and `[Modify]` are kept for a new token. Do not mass-rewrite existing `rulesText` unless asked. `npm test` runs `src/architecture/keyword-agreement.test.ts`, which fails the build on that disagreement.
7. If play itself changed, update `docs/RULEBOOK.md` in the same change. A new token maps to `mark` or `strip`. Do not add an opcode for a verb the glossary does not name.

**New and edited** print uses the whole glossary. User-supplied print stays verbatim. If those words are outside the glossary, the card is not finished, and the supplied words stay intact.
